---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout>
  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#FAFAFA] flex flex-col">

    <!-- Navigation -->
    <nav id="navbar" class="fixed top-0 left-0 right-0 z-50 bg-[#FAFAFA] px-8 py-6 flex justify-between items-center transition-shadow duration-300">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto w-full flex justify-between items-center">
        <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-2 group">
          <div class="w-8 h-8 bg-[#2563EB] rounded-lg flex items-center justify-center group-hover:bg-[#F97316] transition-colors duration-300">
            <span class="font-mono text-white text-sm font-bold">SR</span>
          </div>
          <span class="font-mono text-[#1A1A2E] text-sm font-medium hidden md:block">sroberts.dev</span>
        </a>
        <div class="flex gap-8 text-sm font-medium text-[#6B7280]">
          <a href="/about" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">About</a>
          <a href="/projects" class="text-[#1A1A2E]">Projects</a>
          <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Blog</a>
          <a href="/#contact" class="hover:text-[#1A1A2E] transition-colors">Contact</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="flex-grow">

      <!-- Back link + header -->
      <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pt-40 pb-12">
        <a href="/projects/this-website" class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm tracking-wider hover:underline">← Back to This Website</a>
        <p class="font-mono text-[#F97316] text-sm mt-6 mb-2 tracking-wider">Build Log</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-[#1A1A2E] mb-6 leading-tight">
          Hardening this site's security
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
          Working through Cloudflare's Security Insights report for
          sroberts.dev - what each finding actually means, and what I
          decided to do about it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Article -->
      <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-8 pb-24 space-y-6 text-[#6B7280] leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          This is genuinely a personal portfolio site,
          there's no login form, no user data, nothing especially valuable
          to protect. Even so, I believe that these things matter regardless of
          scale, so it felt worth doing properly rather than skipping it, even if the only value gained is exerience.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>This page will get updated as I work through the rest of the
          report and beyond.</em>
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS)</h2>
        <p>
          Without HSTS, there's a brief window on someone's first visit
          where a man-in-the-middle attacker on an insecure network - public
          wifi etc, - could intercept the connection and downgrade it to
          plain HTTP before the browser knows to insist on encryption. HSTS
          tells browsers "always use HTTPS for this site, no exceptions,"
          closing that window entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          The catch is that it's a one-way commitment for however long you
          set the max-age. Cloudflare is pretty proactive in warning about this, stressing the importance:
          if you ever disable HTTPS, or move to a different host that
          doesn't support it, visitors who've been to your site recently get
          locked out until either that host adds HTTPS or the max-age window
          passes. Since sroberts.dev is on Cloudflare Pages, which serves
          HTTPS automatically, and that I don't expect to be moving, this isn't really
          a practical risk - but it's worth understanding before
          clicking enable, rather than skipping the warning.
        </p>
        <p>
          Configured with a 6 month max-age, subdomains included, preload
          left off for now (this will submit the domain to a permanent,
          hard to reverse browser-level list - worth doing later once things
          have been stable for a while).
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</h2>
        <p>
          Some browsers try to be "helpful" by guessing a file's type
          from its content, rather than trusting what the server explicitly
          declares. "MIME-sniffing" behaviour has historically been
          exploited - disguising a malicious script as an innocent file type
          and letting a sniffing browser execute it based on its own guess
          rather than the declared type.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">nosniff</code> header
          tells the browser to trust the declared type and stop guessing -
          closing off that attack surface with zero functional cost or
          downside.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">A few smaller wins</h2>
        <p>
          Alongside HSTS specifically, a handful of other SSL/TLS settings
          got switched on at the same time: <strong>Always Use HTTPS</strong> (redirects
          stray HTTP requests), <strong>TLS 1.3</strong> (the current, most secure
          version), a <strong>minimum TLS version of 1.2</strong> (blocking
          outdated, insecure connection attempts), and <strong>Automatic
          HTTPS Rewrites</strong> (a safety net that'll catch any accidental HTTP links
          in the site's own content).
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1A2E] pt-6">Still on the list</h2>
        <p>
          The rest of the report, not yet configured: a
          <code class="bg-[#F3F4F6] px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">security.txt</code> file
          (a standardised way for researchers to report vulnerabilities
          responsibly), Bot Fight Mode (challenging automated bot traffic),
          and two AI-crawler related toggles that are more of a values
          decision than a security fix. Updates to follow as each one gets
          done.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>

    <!-- Footer -->
    <footer class="bg-[#1A1A2E] border-t border-white/10 py-6">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-8 flex justify-between items-center">
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">© 2026 Scott Roberts</span>
        <span class="font-mono text-[#6B7280] text-sm">sroberts.dev</span>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </main>

  <script>
    const navbar = document.getElementById('navbar');
    window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      if (window.scrollY > 10) {
        navbar?.classList.add('shadow-md');
      } else {
        navbar?.classList.remove('shadow-md');
      }
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
